<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold III, Duke of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1351">1351</a>–<a href="/years/1386">1386</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Leopold the Just</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuberg">Treaty of Neuberg</a> of 1379 he and his brother Albert III divided the family lands, Leopold taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the western territories later called Further Austria, founding the Leopoldine line. He acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, but was killed fighting the Swiss Confederates at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Battle of Sempach</a> in 1386.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria.jpg" alt="Leopold III, Duke of Austria" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoni Boys · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold_III_of_Austria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
